<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nabonidus Chronicle</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabonidus">Nabonidus</a> Chronicle is an ancient Babylonian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> text recording the reign of Nabonidus, last king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaldean-empire">Chaldean Empire</a>, and the Persian conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>. It notes repeated years in which the New Year festival was not held because the king was absent. It is a key source for the decline of the Chaldean Empire.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nabonidus-chronicle.jpg" alt="Nabonidus Chronicle" width="320" height="330" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Osama Shukir Muhammed Amin FRCP(Glasg) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nabonidus%27s_faults_and_absences_were_recorded_alongside_events_of_his_reign._By_the_autumn_of_539_BCE,_Babylon_had_surrendered_to_the_army_of_king_Cyrus_to_become_part_of_the_growing_Achaemenid_Empire._From_Babylon,_Iraq,_530-400_BCE.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
